    "body": "A pinned session never leaves its generator, so a slow generator (straggler node, throttled GPU) keeps all its sessions while the others idle.\n\n```python\nStickySessionRoutingStrategy.Config(repin_load_ratio=1.5)\n```\nA session moves to the fallback's choice once its generator holds more than 1.5x the mean in-flight load; the move re-prefills its context. Default `None` keeps today's behavior.\n\n```\nCPU sim on the real router classes, wall time vs sticky routing without the knob, one generator 2x slower:\n  multi-turn, 4 generators:              -8.0%   (plain least-loaded: -10.9%)\n  long-context agentic, 8 generators:    -4.9%\nequal-speed fleets and low load:         -2.7% (agentic) .. +0.1%\n```\nRe-pins cost prefix hits: on an equal-speed agentic fleet 0.892 -> 0.864 (314 re-pins per run), at low-load multi-turn 0.879 -> 0.840.\n\nYou may ask: why not plain least-loaded, which is faster with a slow generator? It drops session affinity on every turn, so turns re-prefill on any fleet; the ratio moves only sessions on an overloaded generator.\nYou may ask: why one ratio, not `max(ratio * mean, mean + abs)`? The two-knob version was weaker in every sim case (slow multi-turn -5.0%, slow agentic 0%).\n\n**Test plan**\n`pytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu`: 435 passed, 5 skipped (main: 432 passed; the 2 `test_verifiers_terminal_bench.py` failures are on main too), including `test_sticky_session_repins_off_an_overloaded_generator`.\n\n",
